== THE DM ONE-PAGER
============================================================

{Everything below is repeated in full later. This page exists so that you are not
flipping through eight acts at the table. If you print one page of this document,
print this one.}

  THE THREE THINGS THAT MAKE THIS CHAPTER WORK
    1. The horror is sincere. The comedy is accidental. Never blend them on purpose.
    2. The Gnome is rolled in the OPEN, every scene, and you never fudge it.
    3. Alice cannot be beaten tonight. Run Act 7 so they believe otherwise.

  INITIATIVE
    MAD HATTER    roll normally. d4 hat at the START of each of his turns.
    CLOCKWORKER   acts on 30, first, every round. Lair action on 20.
    ALICE         acts on 15. ONE decision per turn. Never two. House acts with her.

  DCs AT A GLANCE
    5 trivial | 10 easy | 12-13 most Investigation/History | 15-16 stealth & grapples
    18 talking the Queen round | DO NOT ROLL anything Alice decides

  PUZZLE ANSWERS IN ONE LINE EACH
    Wardens   ask either "which gate would the OTHER send me to?", then take the other.
    Court     back player calls parity of the hearts he can see; the rest deduce.
    Teacups   binary search, three questions, eight cups.
    Ropes     A lit both ends + B one end; when A dies light B's far end. 30+15=45.
    Bridge    1+2 over, 1 back, 5+10 over, 2 back, 1+2 over. 17 minutes.
    Dolls     3 groups of 3, weigh A vs B, then 1 vs 1. Two weighings.

  TRACK THESE ON A SCRAP OF PAPER ALL NIGHT
    [ ] Who sent character details ahead   [ ] Was the cave mouth sealed
    [ ] Did the Queen get her heart back   [ ] Whose name the Queen kept
    [ ] Who pulled the pin                 [ ] Gnome results used (no repeats)
    [ ] Unmirror charges (3)               [ ] Notice track (6)
    [ ] Has the Name of a Door been read

  IF YOU ARE RUNNING LONG - cut the Act 4 teacups and the Act 5 bridge. NEVER cut
  Act 6; the party needs the gifts and the briefing or Act 7 is unfair.

  IF THE TABLE GOES SOMEWHERE YOU DID NOT WRITE - ask the Gnome. Out loud. In front
  of them. It answers yes/no truthfully about the literal question, and it will buy
  you the twenty seconds you need to invent the rest.

============================================================
== HOW TO RUN THIS CHAPTER
============================================================

{Read this whole section before the session. Everything after it is written so you
can play straight down the page, top to bottom, without preparing anything else.}

FORMAT KEY
  [READ ALOUD] ... [/READ ALOUD]   Read this to the table, word for word if you like.
  [DM NOTE] ... [/DM NOTE]         Never read this out. This is what is really going on.
  {curly brackets}                 A quick aside to you mid-scene. Do not read.
  [CHECK] skill DC n               A roll, with the success and failure branch written out.
  [LOOT]                           What they find.
  [map:blackfen-cave-map|The Cave - Blackfen]   Double-click any link like this to open
                                   the battle map. Try this one now, before the session.
  === SCENE: ===                   A unit of play. Each one has a Goal so you know when to move on.
  >> CHOICE <<                     A fork with two or three real options. Never punish the third one.

TONE DIAL
  This is a horror chapter that keeps giggling at the wrong moments. The rule is:
  the horror is always sincere, the comedy is always accidental. Nothing in Wonderland
  is TRYING to be funny. The Gnome conjures a grand piano over a monster's head with
  the same blank face it has when a child is being flayed. That contrast is the whole
  flavour. If the table laughs, let them, then immediately show them something that
  makes the laugh curdle.

  Say the word "Alice" rarely. Every NPC flinches from it. By hour four the players
  should be lowering their own voices when they say it, without you ever asking them to.

PACING - a 6 to 9 hour table
  Act 0   The Light at the End                 20-30 min
  Act 1   The Ash Garden (the Gnome)           45-60 min
  Act 2   The Quiet Village + the Gate         60-75 min   {first puzzle}
  Act 3   The Heartless Queen                  75-90 min   {card game + siege, splits the party}
  Act 4   The Mad Hatter                       45-60 min   {big fight}
  Act 5   Inside the Hat: the Clocktower       90-120 min  {two puzzles, boss}
  Act 6   The Cheshire's Mirrors               30-40 min   {breather, gear up}
  Act 7   The Dollhouse                        75-90 min   {stealth horror, finale}
  Act 8   Out                                  15-20 min

  Natural break points: end of Act 3 (before the Hatter) and end of Act 5 (after the
  clock). If you are running two sessions instead of one long one, cut at Act 5.

WHO THIS IS BALANCED FOR
  Five or six characters at 3rd to 5th level. Every fight below has a "SCALING" line.
  {Your table is six level-3s. Use the "as written" numbers. The Cheshire's gifts in
  Act 6 are the equaliser for Act 7 - do not skip that scene, and do not let them
  reach Alice without the gifts.}

THE ONE RULE OF THIS WORLD - tell the players this out loud in Act 6, not before:
  Wonderland is flexible reality. It bends to whoever imagines hardest. Alice imagines
  hardest. That is the only reason she rules it.
  {Mechanically this is your permission slip. If a player describes something clever
  and vivid, let it work, and let the world be a little more theirs for it. If a player
  is timid, the world is stone. Reward imagination literally.}

FREEDOM vs RAILS
  Fixed points (these WILL happen): they arrive; the Hatter is freed and fought; the
  pin comes out of the clock; the Cheshire returns; Alice is dissolved by the Gnome.
  Everything else is negotiable - order, method, who does what, and whether they ever
  learn why any of it happened. If the party finds a sideways solution to a scene,
  take it. There are three or four written routes to every locked door in here and
  the fifth one they invent is better than all of them.

CONTENT NOTE FOR THE TABLE - say this before you start:
  Body horror, mutilation, stitched mouths, harm to a child-shaped thing, and one
  animal death described in detail. Nothing sexual. Ask if anyone wants a line drawn
  and honour it silently.

