== ACT 0 - THE LIGHT AT THE END
============================================================

=== SCENE: THE RUNNING ===
Location: the deep cave, wherever last session left them
Present: the party, and whatever was chasing them
Goal: get them through the light with their hearts already going

[map:blackfen-cave-map|The Cave - Blackfen]

{Open cold. Do not recap. Recap afterwards, over the first campfire. Start the
session mid-sprint - it sets the tempo for the whole chapter.}

[READ ALOUD]
You have been running long enough that your legs have stopped asking permission.
The tunnel narrows, then narrows again, and the sound behind you - that wet,
patient, unhurried sound - has not got any further away.

And then: light.

Ahead, past a throat of broken stone, there is daylight. Real daylight, the kind
you have not seen in three days. It is red. You tell yourself that is the sunset.
[/READ ALOUD]

[DM NOTE]
It is not the sunset. It is never anything else in there.
Whatever is chasing them does not follow through the gap. It stops about forty feet
back and makes a noise that, if you listen honestly, is a whimper. Let a character
with a high passive Perception notice that the thing hunting them is afraid of what
is ahead of them. Do not explain it. Never explain it.
[/DM NOTE]

[CHECK] Athletics or Acrobatics DC 10 (to squeeze the last gap at a dead run)
  Success: through, upright, weapon still in hand.
  Failure: through, on your face, 1d4 bludgeoning. No other penalty.
  {Nobody fails their way out of Act 0. This roll exists so the first die of the
  night hits the table before any exposition does.}

>> CHOICE << at the mouth of the cave:
  A) Run straight out into the light.
  B) Hold the gap and fight the thing behind them.
     {It will not come. After two rounds of nothing they hear it walking AWAY. That
     is more frightening than a fight and it costs you nothing to run.}
  C) Seal the gap - collapse it, spike it, ward it.
     {Let this work completely, first try, no roll. Then in Act 8, when they need to
     come back out this way, remember that they sealed it. Do not warn them now. Do
     not gloat later either - just let it quietly be one of the reasons Act 8 is tense.}

------------------------------------------------------------

=== SCENE: OUT ===
Location: a hillside above a dead forest
Present: everyone
Goal: the establishing shot. Take your time. This is the thesis of the chapter.

[READ ALOUD]
You come out onto a hillside, and the world is wrong in a way that takes a few
seconds to name.

The sky is red. Not sunset-red - flat, even, wall-to-wall red, like the inside of
something. There is no sun in it anywhere. The light arrives from every direction at
once and casts no shadows, which is why each of you keeps glancing down at your own
feet and finding nothing there.

Below you, a forest. Every tree in it is black and dead and perfectly upright, and
they are all precisely the same height, as though someone drew them with a ruler and
never went back to fix it.

The ground is grey and gives underfoot like flour. It is not soil. When you kneel to
touch it, it is warm.

And it is silent. No birds, no wind, no insects. The silence has a pressure to it,
the way deep water does.

Somewhere very far off, a bell rings once. Nothing answers it.
[/READ ALOUD]

[DM NOTE]
The grey ground is ash. Sixty years of it. Do not say so.
If someone spends a full minute searching it: [CHECK] Investigation DC 13 - packed
into the ash they find a child's shoe, a bird's skull the size of a fist, and the
handle off a wooden teacup. All three worn smooth, like river stones.

Nobody casts a shadow anywhere in Wonderland. Mention it twice more across the night,
offhand, then never again. It is not a puzzle. It is a temperature.
[/DM NOTE]

[CHECK] Survival DC 12 (which way?)
  Success: three directions are walkable.
    - Downhill into the dead forest, where something small is standing very still
      among the trunks. {The Gnome.}
    - Along the ridge, towards a thin smear of chimney smoke. {The village.}
    - Towards a distant red-black silhouette on the horizon: a castle, spade-shaped,
      absurdly tall, with a road running to it that is visibly crowded. {The Queen.}
  Failure: every direction looks identical. Try again after ten minutes of walking.
    Meanwhile they hear, very distinctly, a small ceramic CLICK behind them.
    When they turn: nothing there.

{Whichever they pick, the Gnome finds them first - see Act 1. That is not a rail.
The Gnome is simply everywhere, and it has decided about them.}


============================================================
== ACT 1 - THE ASH GARDEN
============================================================

{TONE THIS ACT: nobody has a shadow. Mention it once, offhand, while they are looking
at something else. Do not let anyone investigate it. The word "Alice" has not been
said out loud yet by anyone in the world, and it should not be said tonight until the
village teaches them why.}

=== SCENE: SOMETHING SMALL IN THE TREES ===
Location: the black forest at the foot of the hill
Present: the party, the Garden Gnome
Goal: introduce the Gnome, establish that it is funny, then establish that it is not

[map:forest-clearing-map|The Black Forest]

[READ ALOUD]
The trees are not wood. Up close, the black bark has a grain like burnt sugar and it
crumbles when you lean on it. Nothing has leaves. Nothing has ever had leaves.

In a clearing about thirty feet ahead, standing in the ash with its back to you,
there is a garden gnome.

It is eleven inches tall. Red pointed hat. Blue coat, gold buttons. One boot chipped
down to the white clay underneath. It is the single most ordinary object any of you
have seen in three days and it is, for that reason, deeply upsetting.

As you watch, it turns around. Not by walking. It is simply facing you now.

Its painted face is a smile.
[/READ ALOUD]

[DM NOTE]
The Gnome will not speak, fight on command, follow instructions, or explain itself.
It will follow the party for the entire chapter, and it can never be left behind - if
they abandon it, it is simply standing in the next room, in the ash, in the mirror,
on the tea table, ahead of them.

TEACH THE THREE THINGS, in this order, in the first ten minutes:

1. IT DOES SOMETHING RANDOM.
   Roll on the GNOME TABLE (Appendix A) in the open, right now, before anyone speaks.
   Whatever comes up, describe it perfectly flatly. A grand piano appears thirty feet
   in the air above a dead tree and detonates it. A live trout falls into Brann's
   hood. Every button on the Gnome's coat plays a single note of a lullaby.
   Then: CLICK. The face is a frown. Then: CLICK. Smiling again.

2. IT ANSWERS YES AND NO.
   The moment any player asks the Gnome a question, do this:
     - If the question is a yes/no question, the Gnome clicks to :) for yes or :( for
       no, and IT IS ALWAYS TRUTHFUL. Always. This never breaks, all night.
     - If the question is anything else - "who are you", "what happened here",
       "where do we go" - the face cycles :) :( :) :( four or five times, very fast,
       and stops on whichever it started on. That is the Gnome shrugging.
   Do not tell the players this rule. Let them discover it and say it out loud
   themselves. When they do, nod once and say nothing.
   {It answers only the LITERAL question. "Is it safe?" is a different question from
   "Will we survive it?" Players who learn to word questions carefully are playing
   this chapter correctly. Reward precision brutally in both directions.}

3. IT IS NOT SAFE.
   About five minutes after they've started enjoying it, roll the Gnome table again
   and pick, deliberately, one of the horrible results. A jet of boiling tea. A swarm
   of moths made of paper with faces printed on them. Have it land on the PARTY. Hurt
   somebody for 2d6. The Gnome's face does not change at all while it happens.
   That is the moment the table stops treating it as a mascot. That is the point.
[/DM NOTE]

[CHECK] Arcana DC 15 (what IS it?)
  Success: "There is no spell on this thing, and no spell in it. Magic is not being
    CAST here - it is leaking. This is what a hole in the world looks like when
    someone has painted a face on it."
  Failure: "It's a lawn ornament. Absolutely nothing about it is magical." {This is
    also true, and that is worse.}

[CHECK] Insight DC 16 (watching the face)
  Success: the smile and the frown are not moods. They are answers. It has been
    waiting for someone to ask it something.
  Failure: nothing. It is a painted face.

>> CHOICE << what do they do with it?
  A) Take it with them. {It permits this. It weighs eleven pounds, which is far too
     much, and whoever carries it will mention that all night.}
  B) Leave it. {It is in the next scene, standing in the road, facing them.}
  C) Break it. {Let them. Roll d20: on 1-19 the pieces are gone from their hands and
     it is standing behind them, intact, frowning. On a 20 it stays broken for one
     hour of game time and the party has no Gnome for the Quiet Village, which is
     substantially more dangerous. Then it comes back. It is never angry about this.}

