=== SCENE: THE ORCHARD OF THE FIRST TRAVELLER ===
Location: deeper in, where the trees are planted in rows
Present: the party
Goal: the first real lore drop, entirely optional, entirely skippable

[map:open-field-map|The Orchard of the First Traveller]

{This scene is here so that a curious party gets rewarded and an impatient party
loses nothing. If they walk past it, cut it. The information reappears in Act 6.}

[READ ALOUD]
The trees begin to fall into rows. This was an orchard once. In the middle of it
there is a low stone bench, and beside the bench a signpost with four arms, and every
arm has been snapped off flush except one, which reads, in a neat and cheerful hand:

    THIS WAY TO EVERYTHING

It points straight up.
[/READ ALOUD]

[CHECK] Investigation DC 12 (the bench)
  Success: carved into the underside, upside down, where you would only see it lying
    on your back beneath it:
      "I have written it all down. Someone will come. Someone always comes.
       There are notes hidden in every one of us. Find the last one. - T."
  Failure: it's a bench.

[DM NOTE]
"T" is the First Traveller. The party does not need to know more than this all night.
What they will eventually learn, in Act 6:

  The First Traveller came down here before Alice did. He mapped Wonderland, wrote
  down what it was and how it actually WORKS, and split the record into notes hidden
  in the world's residents. He was the one who worked out that reality here bends to
  whoever imagines hardest. Alice found that out from him and then made sure nobody
  else would.

  The Last Note is the final page. It is the page that says how to leave, and what is
  waiting on the other side. Alice keeps it. She keeps it because it is the only
  thing in Wonderland she is frightened of, and she likes having it close.

  DO NOT let the party read the note this session. That is next chapter. Let them
  hold it. Let them look at the first line. Then get them out.
[/DM NOTE]

[LOOT]
  Coins: 0
  Items: a brass signpost arm, snapped off, engraved THIS WAY TO EVERYTHING.
    {Pure flavour, but a clever player can use it in Act 6 as a component for a door.
    If they do, say yes.}

============================================================
== ACT 2 - THE QUIET VILLAGE
============================================================

=== SCENE: THE PEOPLE WITH NO MOUTHS ===
Location: a village of leaning houses under the red sky
Present: forty-odd villagers
Goal: horror. Not a fight, not a puzzle. Let it be unbearable and then let them leave.

[map:greenhollow-square|The Quiet Village]

[READ ALOUD]
It is a village. That is the worst part - it is an ordinary village, thatch and
whitewash and a well in the square, and washing hung out to dry under a sky the
colour of a wound.

There are people here. Perhaps forty. They are going about their evening: a man
carrying a bucket, two women turning a mattress, a boy chasing a hoop with a stick.

Every one of them has had their mouth sewn shut.

The stitching is neat. Black thread, evenly spaced, six or seven loops each, done
carefully, done by hand, some of it long healed and some of it fresh and weeping.
The boy with the hoop has it too. His stitches are the newest.

They see you. All of them, at once, the way birds turn.

And then, very calmly, without hurrying, without a sound, every single one of them
walks indoors and closes the door.

The boy leaves his hoop rolling. It goes on rolling for a long time, and falls over,
and the sound it makes when it falls over is the loudest thing in the village.
[/READ ALOUD]

[DM NOTE]
Sit in the silence. Do not fill it. Let a player break it.

The villagers are not hostile, cursed, or undead. They are people. They sewed their
own mouths shut - or their neighbours did, for them, kindly. It is the only reliable
protection: Alice is drawn to interesting speech. Silence is camouflage.

RULES OF THE VILLAGE (apply consistently, never announce them):
  - No villager will open a door while a player is speaking out loud.
  - If the party goes entirely silent - gestures, notes, chalk, mime - doors open.
    This is the puzzle of this scene and it is a soft one. Do not hint at it for at
    least ten minutes. Let them try shouting first. Let shouting fail.
  - Say the word "Alice" out loud anywhere in the village and EVERY door in earshot
    slams, and a woman upstairs starts hammering nails into her own window frame.
    Use this exactly once. It teaches the lesson for the rest of the night.
[/DM NOTE]

>> CHOICE << how do they make contact?
  A) Knock, talk, demand. {Nothing. All night. No villager ever answers a voice.}
  B) Go silent and write, draw, or gesture.
     {A door opens four inches. An old woman - grey, exhausted, stitched - holds out
     a slate and a nub of chalk. She will answer written questions in writing. She
     writes slowly and in small letters, as if even her handwriting is trying to be
     quiet. Her name is MARGARET.}
  C) Force a door. {It opens. The family inside kneel down and hold still, eyes
     closed, waiting to be hurt, and do not resist anything at all. This is worse for
     the players than any fight. Let them feel it. No mechanical penalty. None needed.}
  D) Ask the GNOME yes/no questions about the village. {Fully valid and it works.
     This is where most tables realise what the Gnome is for. Answer honestly:
     "Are these people dangerous?" :( ... "Are they afraid of us?" :( ...
     "Are they afraid of something else?" :) ... "Is it here?" :( ... "Will it come?" :) }

MARGARET'S SLATE - what she will write, one line at a time, if asked in silence:
  - "We are not permitted to be interesting."
  - "She hears clever things. Do not be clever near her."
  - "The Queen's castle takes people in. Some of them. If your papers are in order."
  - "Do not go to the tea house. Nobody comes back from a party."
  - "The cat is dead. She has it on the table. She keeps it dead ON PURPOSE."
  - If asked about the Gnome, she looks at it for a long moment and writes:
    "That was here first. That was here before HER." Then she rubs it out fast.
  - If asked how to leave: she writes "the last note", stops, stares at what she has
    written, and scrubs the slate so hard the chalk snaps.

[LOOT]
  Coins: they will not accept money. If pressed, Margaret writes: "Nothing to buy."
  Items: if the party gives the village something - food, a blanket, healing, a name
    written down - Margaret gives them a SPOOL OF BLACK THREAD, and writes:
    "For your mouths. When she looks at you."
    {This is real and it is mechanical. See Appendix B: Margaret's Thread. It is one
    of only three ways to survive being noticed in Act 7. Give it out with weight.}

------------------------------------------------------------

=== SCENE: THE ROAD OF PETITIONERS ===
Location: the road up to the spade-shaped castle
Present: a queue of the desperate; two wardens at the gate
Goal: the first hard puzzle, and the moment that splits the party

[map:bandit-camp-map|The Road of Petitioners]

[READ ALOUD]
The road to the castle is packed.

Hundreds of people are queued along it, stretching back a quarter-mile, and none of
them are talking, because none of them can. They stand in a line under a red sky
holding pieces of paper. Some of the papers are beautiful - illuminated, ribboned,
sealed. Some are scraps of bark. One woman near the front is holding out both hands,
palms up, with nothing in them at all, and she has clearly been standing there for
a very long time.

At the gate, under a portcullis shaped like a heart with a bite out of it, there are
two guards. They are enormous and identical and they are, unmistakably, twins: round,
pink, jacketed, buttoned to the chin. Their mouths are NOT sewn. That is the first
thing you notice about them, and it is somehow the most alarming thing on this road.

The left one says, cheerfully: "Papers, please!"
The right one says, cheerfully: "Papers, please!"
[/READ ALOUD]

[DM NOTE]
These are DUM and DEE. They are the gate protocol made flesh, and they are the puzzle.

THE SITUATION, which they will explain freely and honestly if asked:
  - There are two doors under the portcullis. One leads INTO the castle and the
    audience chamber. The other leads into the KENNEL, which is where the Queen's
    unfed things are, and which is a death sentence.
  - Each guard stands at one door.
  - ONE of the twins always tells the truth. The OTHER always lies. They will not say
    which is which and neither will admit anything about the other.
  - The party may ask ONE guard ONE yes/no question. Total. Not one each. One.
  - "Those who sent word ahead" - see below - are waved through without any of this.

THE SOLUTION (the classic, and there are several valid forms - accept any of them):
  Ask either guard: "If I asked the OTHER guard whether YOUR door is the way in,
  would he say yes?"
    - If the answer is NO, the door you asked at is the way in.
