=== SCENE: THE GAME OF COURT ===
Goal: the puzzle. Budget 30-40 minutes. It is worth every one of them.

[READ ALOUD]
"The game is Court," she says. "It is very old and very simple and I have not lost it
in sixty years, which I would enjoy more if anyone had come close.

Five of you will stand in a line, facing my throne. One behind the other. Each of you
will wear a card upon your brow, placed there by my soldiers, and you will not see
your own card, not ever, not by any means. You will see every card in front of you.
You will see nothing behind you.

There are only two suits in this deck. Hearts, and Spades.

I will begin at the back and I will work forward. Each of you, in turn, will say one
word. That word will be 'Hearts' or that word will be 'Spades' and it will be the
suit of the card on your own brow. It will be one of those two words and nothing
else. You may not sigh, or wink, or lean, or cough, or emphasise, or weep. One word.

You may confer for one minute before the first card is placed. After that, silence.

Be right, and go free. Be wrong, and I keep your name."

She says the last part the way a clerk reads a fee schedule.
[/READ ALOUD]

[DM NOTE]
THE RULES, on the table, exactly as written:
  1. Five participants in a line, facing away from the back of the line. Each sees
     every card in front of them and none behind.
  2. Two suits only: Hearts and Spades. Any mix. They are not told how many of each.
  3. The Queen asks from BACK to FRONT. Back one goes first.
  4. Each says exactly one word: "Hearts" or "Spades".
  5. Everyone hears every answer.
  6. One minute to confer beforehand. Nothing after.
  7. Wrong answer: the Queen keeps that character's NAME. See the cost below.

THE SOLUTION - the parity code:
  The person at the BACK cannot deduce their own card. They are a sacrifice, and
  their job is to spend their one word as a SIGNAL rather than an answer.
  Agree beforehand: the back player counts the Hearts they can see in front of them.
    - Odd number of Hearts  -> they say "Hearts"
    - Even number of Hearts -> they say "Spades"
  The back player has a coin-flip chance of being right by luck.
  EVERY OTHER PLAYER IS THEN CERTAIN. The fourth player counts the Hearts in front of
  them and compares with the parity the back player announced; the difference is
  their own card. The third does the same, accounting for what the fourth said. And
  so on to the front, who has heard everything and seen nothing, and knows exactly.

  Guaranteed: 4 of 5. At risk: 1 of 5, on a coin flip.

RUNNING IT AT THE TABLE - do this physically, it is so much better:
  Use real playing cards. Deal five face down. Have the five players hold their card
  to their forehead without looking. You, the DM, describe what each can see, or just
  let them look at each other, which is more fun and completely fair.
  Then run the round for real. Their code either works or it does not.
  DO NOT COACH. If they solve it, it will be the best twenty minutes of the session.

IF THEY CANNOT CRACK IT - escalating help, spaced ten minutes apart:
  Hint 1 (from the Queen, bored): "The one at the back cannot win. Stop trying to make
    him win. Ask instead what he might be worth."
  Hint 2 (from a card soldier, whispering, a Spade who is tired of this): "Counting.
    They always forget they are allowed to count."
  Hint 3 (from the GNOME): if any player asks it yes/no questions about the code they
    are designing - "Will this plan save four of us?" - it answers honestly. This is
    the intended fallback and it is not cheating, it is the tool working.
  Hint 4: let them play it out wrong. Losing this is survivable and interesting.
    See below.

THE COST OF LOSING - "I keep your name":
  This is real and it is not a death sentence. If a character answers wrong:
    - The Queen writes their name in a ledger and closes it, and from that moment on
      NO NPC IN WONDERLAND CAN SAY OR PERCEIVE THAT NAME. Other players still can.
      Written records of them blur. Introductions fail. It is lonely and it is eerie
      and you should play it every single time it would come up.
    - AND: a nameless thing is very hard to notice. That character has ADVANTAGE on
      every Stealth check in Act 7, and Alice's Notice mechanic (Appendix C) takes an
      extra failed roll to trigger against them.
  {Losing this puzzle makes the endgame EASIER and the roleplay sadder. That is a
  good trade and it means you never have to fudge the outcome. Let the dice fall.}
[/DM NOTE]

