=== SCENE: THE CARD AT THE FRONT ===
Goal: the twist. This is the emotional peak of Act 3.

[DM NOTE]
Here is the thing you have not told them.

THE CARD ON THE FRONT PLAYER'S FOREHEAD IS THE QUEEN OF HEARTS.

It is not a normal card. It is her heart. Alice cut it out, pressed it flat, printed
a face on it, and slipped it back into the Queen's own deck, where the Queen would
shuffle it and deal it and hand it out and never once be able to see it, because
nobody can see their own heart. It has been in circulation for sixty years.

EVERY OTHER PLAYER IN THE LINE CAN SEE IT. They are looking straight at it. And under
the rules they may say one word, and that word must be "Hearts" or "Spades", so they
cannot tell the front player what it is.

WHEN THE FRONT PLAYER'S TURN COMES, the Queen adds one line she has not mentioned
before. She says it flatly, and every card soldier in the hall turns its head:

  "You at the front. You may say your suit, as the others did. Or you may name your
   card exactly - suit and rank, both. Name it right and I will grant you a thing
   beyond this game. Name it wrong and I keep your name, and I keep your voice."

HOW THE PLAYER CAN POSSIBLY KNOW:
  a) The GNOME. "Is my card the Queen of Hearts?" - :) . This is the intended route
     and it is the pay-off for two hours of learning what the Gnome is. If a player
     does this, do not hesitate for one second. Reward it enormously.
  b) Deduction from the room: [CHECK] Investigation DC 15, allowed to anyone who has
     been paying attention to the hall - "there is not one red card in this room,
     among forty soldiers, and she has been dealing from a deck that is short exactly
     one card, and there is a hole in her chest the size of a fist."
  c) A player simply GUESSES it because the scene is written well enough that guessing
     it is a reasonable leap. If they guess it: yes. Absolutely yes. That is the best
     possible version of this.
  d) The other players in the line breaking the rules to tell them. Let them try. The
     card soldiers will physically stop it - a Spade's halberd comes down across the
     mouth. But if a player finds a way that does not involve speech, sound, or
     movement... say yes, and let the Queen pretend not to have noticed.

WHEN IT IS NAMED CORRECTLY - read this:
[/DM NOTE]

[READ ALOUD]
You say it. "The Queen of Hearts."

Nothing happens for about two seconds.

Then the card on your forehead goes warm - unpleasantly warm, blood warm - and it is
not a card any more, it is soft and wet and it is beating, and it is far too big to
have ever been a card, and it is in your hand.

Every soldier in the hall drops to one knee at once. It sounds like a building
falling over.

The Queen has not moved. Her hands are on the arms of the throne and her knuckles
are white and her face has not changed at all, because it has not been able to change
in sixty years.

She says, very carefully, as though the words are heavy: "Bring it here. Please."

And then she says: "Please." Again. Like she is testing the word out.
[/READ ALOUD]

[DM NOTE]
If they give it back:
  She puts it in the hole in her chest herself, with both hands, badly, the way you
  would repack a bag in a hurry. The handkerchief flutters to the floor.
  Then she FEELS things, all sixty years of them, in about four seconds, and it is
  awful to watch. She screams once - the first sound of real feeling anyone in this
  world has made in decades, and every window in the castle cracks - and then she
  sits back down and weeps like a person, not like a queen.
  Rewards: the JOKER, freely given. Plus the CROQUET MALLET OF THE FIRST GAME
  (Appendix B). Plus ten card soldiers who will fight for the party ONCE, at a moment
  of the party's choosing, if they can get word to the castle. {They can. In Act 8.}
  She also says the single most useful thing anyone says all night:
    "The cat had a note in it. She gutted it looking for the note. She did not find
     the note, because the cat was cleverer than her about exactly one thing, and it
     is the only reason any of us still have a chance."

If they keep it:
  She does not fight them for it. She simply stops. She says "Ah." and sits back and
  looks at the ceiling, and the card soldiers begin, very quietly, to leave the room.
  Rewards: they keep a beating heart that grants advantage on saves against fear
  while held - and the Joker, which the Queen tosses at their feet without looking.
