=== SCENE: THE EIGHT CUPS (optional - offer it, do not require it) ===
Goal: give clever parties a way to gut the boss fight before it starts

[DM NOTE]
Before the Hatter attacks, he does what he always does: he pours.

Eight cups appear in front of the party, in a row, numbered 1 to 8 by the little
painted numerals on the saucers. He announces, with enormous warmth:

  "Seven of these will kill you. ONE of them is TEA. Drink with me and I will hear
   you out - I have not been heard out in sixty years and I should like it very much.
   Refuse, and we shall skip to the part where I hurt you, which I shall also enjoy,
   but less."

THE PUZZLE: identify the safe cup.
  The Gnome is standing on the table. It has been standing on the table since before
  the table arrived. The players may ask it yes/no questions and it cannot lie.
  BUT the Hatter permits only THREE questions - "three is the number of questions at
  a tea party, everyone knows that" - and eight cups is more than three.

  SOLUTION: binary search. Three yes/no questions distinguish exactly eight things.
    Q1: "Is the tea in cups 1 to 4?"   -> splits 8 into 4
    Q2: "Is it in cups 1 and 2?" (or 5 and 6) -> splits 4 into 2
    Q3: "Is it cup 1?" (or whichever) -> splits 2 into 1
  Any equivalent halving works. Accept clumsy phrasings that still halve.
  {Watch for the classic error - a player asking "Is it cup 3?", "Is it cup 5?",
  "Is it cup 7?" That is three questions for a 3-in-8 chance. Let them do it if they
  insist. Do not warn them. The Gnome will answer :( three times with total serenity
  and the table will groan and it will be a better lesson than any hint.}

  ALSO ACCEPT: a paladin's Divine Sense, a Detect Poison and Disease, an antitoxin
  chugged in advance, a familiar drinking first, a druid who simply eats poison for
  breakfast. Any of these bypasses it. Say yes and move on - the puzzle is a gift,
  not a gate.

REWARD FOR DRINKING THE RIGHT CUP:
  The Hatter is DELIGHTED. Genuinely. He sits down. For four minutes he is almost a
  person, and he tells them:
    - "She put a rabbit in my hat. I can feel it running. It has been running for
      sixty years and I cannot get it out and I cannot sleep for the ticking."
    - "The cat is on her table. She keeps it dead the way you keep jam in a jar."
    - "I nearly had her, you know. I was CLOSE." (He is proud of this. It is the last
      real thing left in him.)
    - "You'll want the hat off me. Yes. Well. I can't just GIVE it to you." And then,
      sadly, and this is the important line: "It has to come off. It doesn't have to
      come off a live man, though. That's the only kindness on the menu."
  MECHANICALLY: he begins the fight at HALF hit points, has already used his Teapot,
  and on the round he drops below Bloodied he stops fighting for one full round and
  says "thank you" to whoever poured. That is a huge swing. It should be.

PENALTY FOR DRINKING A WRONG CUP: 4d6 poison damage, Con save DC 14 for half, and the
character is poisoned for the fight. Nobody dies from this - if it would drop them,
they go to 1 hit point and vomit something with legs in it.
[/DM NOTE]

