  5. AT HALF HIT POINTS he stops laughing. Mid-laugh. Read the block below.

THE GNOME IN THIS FIGHT: roll once on the GNOME TABLE at the start of each round and
apply it to a random creature. Yes, including the Hatter. Yes, including the party.
Announce every roll before you know what it does.
[/DM NOTE]

{Read the following aloud the moment the Hatter drops to half hit points.}

[READ ALOUD]
He stops laughing.

Not gradually. The laugh cuts off exactly halfway through, like a door closing on it,
and what is standing on the table now is a very tired man in an absurd hat with two
black stitches where his eyes should be.

He says, in a completely different voice - quiet, hoarse, ordinary:

"Oh. Oh, thank God. Please keep going."

And then the laugh comes back on, all at once, at full volume, and he is gone again.
[/READ ALOUD]

[DM NOTE]
IF THEY TRY TO SAVE HIM: they cannot. Not tonight. Say so, through the Cheshire, in
Act 6, in exactly these words: "You'd need her dead for that, and she isn't yet."
Let a player who really tries carry the failure. That is worth more than a win here.

WHEN HE DIES:
  He does not fall down. He sits down, in the chair at the head of the table, and
  puts his hands in his lap, and the hat comes off on its own and rolls along the
  table and stops in front of the party, brim up, like a bowl.
  Then he is a card again - the Joker, lying on the seat, ink drawn much smaller and
  quieter now. {If a player keeps it, good. The Hatter is not gone. Next chapter.}

  And out of the mouth of the hat, faintly, from a very long way down, comes ticking.
[/DM NOTE]

[LOOT]
  Coins: 0
  Items: - THE HAT (see Appendix B). It is a door.
         - The Joker card, now quiet.
         - A teaspoon. There are three hundred of them, but this one is warm, and it
