== ACT 5 - INSIDE THE HAT
============================================================

{TONE THIS ACT: by now the players should be lowering their own voices when they say
the name. If they are not, have the Rabbit stop dead mid-sentence the first time one
of them says it, and stare, and then carry on counting. Do it once. Shadows: inside
the hat there ARE shadows, and they are all wrong ones - too many, too long, and none
of them belonging to anybody present. Mention it and move on.}

=== SCENE: DOWN ===
Location: the inside of a hat
Present: everyone who steps in
Goal: establish the Inventory World and its rules in ninety seconds of dread

[READ ALOUD]
The hat is two feet across at the brim and the hole in the middle of it is dark in a
way that hurts to look into for too long.

You step in.

There is no falling. That is the first wrong thing. You simply arrive.

You are standing on a floor made of things. Not a floor WITH things on it - a floor
made of them, compacted, fused: chair legs and dinner plates and doorknobs and shoes
and cutlery and hundreds upon hundreds of hats, pressed flat into a surface you can
walk on. It goes to the horizon in every direction.

Above you there is no sky. There is a CEILING, at about four hundred feet, and it is
made of the same thing, and it is upside down, and if you look at it too long you
become certain that you are the one who is upside down.

The light comes from candles. Thousands of them, at every angle, some burning
upwards, some burning sideways, some burning down. None of them are getting shorter.

And somewhere - everywhere - there is ticking. Not one clock. Thousands, and none of
them agree, and none of them are keeping time with each other, and the sound of it
gets into your back teeth.

In the middle distance, rising out of the floor of objects, there is a tower.
[/READ ALOUD]

[DM NOTE]
THE RULES OF THE INVENTORY WORLD - introduce each by demonstration, not by lecture:

  1. NOTHING IS LOST HERE. Everything anyone in Wonderland has ever misplaced is on
     this floor. A player who searches for a specific ordinary object FINDS it, with
     no roll, within one minute. Keys. Rope. A spoon. A ladder. Lamp oil.
     {This is a gift. Let them use it. It makes the puzzles in this act solvable by
     resourceful players and it is genuinely delightful the first three times.}
     What they cannot find: anything unique, anything alive, anything they have not
     lost themselves or seen lost.

  2. TIME IS LOCAL. Different regions of this world run at different speeds, and the
     boundaries are visible as faint shimmering seams in the air, like heat haze.
     Cross a seam and you can watch your own party moving at half speed or double
     behind you. Mechanically, when you want tension: crossing a seam costs a
     character 1 round of "lag" - they act on the round AFTER they arrive.

  3. THINGS THAT WERE PUT IN HERE ARE STILL IN HERE. Including the things Alice put
     in. See the Sinister Inventory below.

  4. THE PARTY CANNOT LEAVE THE WAY THEY CAME. The brim of the hat, seen from inside,
     is a circle of red sky about a mile up, and it is closing at roughly a foot an
     hour. Tell them this in the first five minutes. It is a clock and they should
     hear it ticking from the start. {In truth Act 5's ending overrides this, but
     they must not know that.}

THE SINISTER INVENTORY - roll or pick, one per twenty minutes of wandering:
  1. A SWARM OF LEFT SHOES, moving as one animal. Use SWARM OF RATS. Harmless-looking
     and genuinely dangerous, which is this chapter's whole personality.
  2. THE COAT RACK. Use ANIMATED ARMOR, but it is nine feet tall and hung with the
     coats of everyone who has ever died in Wonderland, and it wants one more.
  3. A DOOR STANDING FREE IN THE OPEN. Locked. Knocking is answered. Do not ever say
     by what. If they open it, they find themselves, six hours older, walking away.
  4. THE COLLECTION. A tidy pile of 4,000 identical brass keys, arranged with obvious
     care by someone who never found the lock. [CHECK] Investigation DC 14: one of
     them is warm. {It opens the clocktower's front door, saving them the climb.
     Give this to any party that stops to look at things.}
  5. THE MIRROR. Face down under a heap of umbrellas. {THIS MATTERS. See Act 6. If
     they find it now, do not explain it. Let them carry a heavy mirror around for
     an hour. It pays off.}
  6. GRANDFATHER CLOCKS, forty of them, standing in a circle facing inwards, all
     showing a different time, all ticking, none of them agreeing. Standing in the
     middle of the circle for more than a minute: DC 13 Wisdom save or take 2d6
     psychic and age visibly - one grey streak, one new line at the eye. Permanent.
     Cosmetic only. Players hate this far more than damage. Use it once.
[/DM NOTE]

