=== SCENE: PUZZLE - THE ROPE ROOM (landing two) ===

[READ ALOUD]
The landing opens into a round room with a locked iron door on the far side, and set
into the door is a thick glass panel showing a mechanism inside: a bolt, held back by
a spring, held in place by a wax seal.

Above the door, painted in the neat cheerful hand you are starting to recognise:

    THE BOLT DRAWS BACK FOR FORTY-FIVE MINUTES OF FIRE.
    NOT A MINUTE MORE. NOT A MINUTE LESS.
    THERE IS NO CLOCK IN THIS ROOM AND THERE NEVER WILL BE.

On a hook beside the door hang two lengths of grey rope, and beneath them a tinderbox.

A brass plate under the hook reads:

    EACH ROPE BURNS FOR EXACTLY ONE HOUR, END TO END.
    NEITHER ROPE BURNS EVENLY. HALF A ROPE IS NOT HALF AN HOUR.
    YOU HAVE TWO ROPES. THERE ARE NO MORE ROPES.
[/READ ALOUD]

[DM NOTE]
THE PUZZLE: measure exactly 45 minutes using two unevenly-burning one-hour ropes.

THE SOLUTION:
  - At time zero: light rope A at BOTH ends, and rope B at ONE end.
  - Rope A, burning from both ends, is consumed in exactly 30 minutes, whatever the
    unevenness, because the two flames together always eat the whole hour's worth of
    rope at double rate.
  - The instant rope A is gone (30 minutes), light the SECOND end of rope B.
    Rope B has 30 minutes of burn left; from both ends that takes 15 minutes.
  - Rope B is gone at 45 minutes exactly. The bolt draws back.

RUNNING IT: the wax seal in the glass panel is your feedback. As soon as they light
anything, the wax begins to soften. If they get it right, at the 45 minute mark the
seal cracks and the bolt slams back. If they get it wrong - too long or too short -
the wax RE-HARDENS and the ropes are restored on their hook, unburnt, as though
nothing happened, and one clock somewhere in the tower gains an hour.
  {THIS PUZZLE HAS INFINITE RETRIES. Say so implicitly by letting the first failure
  reset cleanly. It removes all the pressure and makes the table settle in and
  actually think, which is what you want. The cost of failure is TIME, and time is
  about to matter enormously - see the Rabbit.}

HINTS, spaced out:
  1. The Gnome, if asked yes/no: "Can a rope be lit at both ends?" :) - that single
     question cracks the whole thing for most tables and it is a completely fair use
     of the tool.
  2. A clock on the wall outside the room chimes the half hour. Someone will notice
     that 30 is half of 60 and that half of the remainder is 15.
  3. Direct, from a scratched note on the underside of the hook, if they are stuck
     twenty minutes: "TWO ENDS BURN TWICE AS FAST. - T."

ALTERNATE SOLUTIONS TO ACCEPT: a character with a reliable internal sense of time
(a monk, a warforged, a spell) can simply count it. Say yes. Do not defend the puzzle.
[/DM NOTE]

[LOOT]
  Coins: 0
  Items: a coil of unburnt rope from the reset {it never runs out; it is the world's
    most boring infinite resource and a smart party will find a use for it}

------------------------------------------------------------

=== SCENE: PUZZLE - THE LANTERN BRIDGE (landing three) ===

[READ ALOUD]
The stairs end at the edge of a gap.

The tower's interior opens into a shaft, and the far side is sixty feet away across a
bridge of clock hands - dozens of them, laid flat and lashed together, black iron,
thin, and visibly not very well attached.

There is one lantern. It is the only light. Twenty feet out from either end the dark
is total, and the dark here is not empty; something in it is keeping pace with the
lantern, just outside the edge of it, and you can hear it being careful.

Painted on the last stair, in that same neat hand:

    THE BRIDGE HOLDS TWO. NEVER THREE.
    THE LANTERN DOES NOT THROW. THE LANTERN IS CARRIED.
    WHAT IS IN THE DARK WILL NOT ENTER THE LIGHT.
    AND IN NINETEEN MINUTES THIS SHAFT WILL BE FULL OF BELLS.
[/READ ALOUD]

[DM NOTE]
THE PUZZLE: the whole party must cross. Two at a time, maximum. The lantern must be
carried with any crossing group, and must be walked back for the next group. A pair
crossing together moves at the SLOWER one's pace. Total time available: 19 minutes.

CROSSING TIMES - assign these to the four slowest characters, or use these NPC-ish
labels and let the party sort out who is who:
    Fastest character:  1 minute
    Second:             2 minutes
    Third:              5 minutes
    Slowest:           10 minutes
  {If your party has more than four, the extras cross for free tucked between - or,
  better, give them a time of 2 minutes each and add 2 minutes to the budget per
  extra character. The puzzle's structure is unaffected.}

THE NAIVE SOLUTION (the trap): the fastest shuttles everyone.
    1+10 = 10, back 1, 1+5 = 5, back 1, 1+2 = 2. Total 19... which is EXACTLY the
    budget, so a table that finds this has technically survived - but see below, the
    bells are already ringing as the last foot lands. Let them have it. It is a win.
    {Most tables find this. It is a legitimate answer to the riddle as posed and it
    makes them feel clever. GOOD. If your table finds it fast, tighten the budget to
    17 minutes and let them find the real answer.}

THE ELEGANT SOLUTION (17 minutes) - the two slowest cross TOGETHER:
    1 and 2 cross         -> 2 minutes   (total 2)
    1 walks the lantern back -> 1 minute (total 3)
    5 and 10 cross together  -> 10 minutes (total 13)
    2 walks the lantern back -> 2 minutes (total 15)
    1 and 2 cross            -> 2 minutes (total 17)
  The insight is that the two slow characters should never be spent separately -
  pair them so the 10 and the 5 happen at the same time.

WHAT IS IN THE DARK: do not stat it. It never attacks. It only ever gets CLOSER, and
it is always exactly at the edge of the lantern light, and once - just once, at your
choosing - it should say a player's name, correctly, in that player's own voice.
  If they take longer than the budget: the bells. Every clock in the tower strikes at
  once. Anyone still on the bridge takes 4d6 thunder and must make a DC 14 Dexterity
  save or fall. Falling is not death - they land, badly, forty feet down, in the
  Inventory floor, take 3d6, and have to climb back up alone in the dark, which is
  the actual punishment and it is enough.

HINTS:
  1. "The lantern always has to come back. Who is cheapest to spend on a walk back?"
  2. The Gnome: "Will the two slowest walking together save us time?" :)
  3. If truly stuck: a clock hand in the bridge has scratched into it, "TWO SLOW ONES
     TOGETHER. THEY ONLY COST ONCE. - T."
[/DM NOTE]

