=== ENCOUNTER: THE CLOCKWORKER RABBIT (landing four) ===
Enemies: the Clockworker Rabbit (Appendix C)
Terrain / hazards: the clock room - gears, seams of local time, a 200-foot drop
Tactics: it does not want to kill them. It wants them to be LATE.
Treasure: the pocket clock
Scaling if the party struggles: it stops using Rewind; it uses Haste on the party
  by accident once, and looks appalled

[map:orins-tower-map|The Clock Room]

[READ ALOUD]
The top of the tower is one room and it is full of gears.

They are the size of cartwheels and the size of dinner plates and one of them is the
size of the room, and they are all turning, and they are all turning at different
speeds, and about a third of them are turning the wrong way.

In the middle of it, on a stool that is too small for it, there is a rabbit.

It is four feet tall. It is wearing a waistcoat that used to be very fine. Its fur
was white once. It has the exhausted, over-groomed look of something that has been
holding still for a very long time on purpose.

It is holding a pocket watch in both hands, the way you would hold a bird.

Without looking up, it says:

"You're four minutes early and I hate you for it."
[/READ ALOUD]

[DM NOTE]
TALK FIRST. This is not a fight you should open with initiative. The Rabbit will
converse, at length, in a state of total exhaustion, and everything it says is true:
  - "I am not her servant. Do stop saying that."
  - "She put the clock in my hands and closed my hands. That is the whole of it."
  - "If it stops being stopped, the cat comes back. If the cat comes back, she notices.
    If she notices, she comes HERE. Do you understand what you are asking me to have
    happen to me?"
  - "No. No, you may not have it. I am sorry. I am extremely sorry. No."

>> CHOICE << three routes, all valid:
  A) FIGHT IT. Straightforward. See the statblock. It is a nasty, tricky, mobile
     fight and it is the intended default.
  B) CONVINCE IT. [CHECK] Persuasion DC 20, with ADVANTAGE and a -5 to the DC if the
     party can offer any of the following, which they may well have:
       - the Queen's heart restored (word travels; the Rabbit weeps at this)
       - a promise, sworn on something the Rabbit can verify with the Gnome
       - Margaret's Thread, offered to it {"...for after. For when she looks at me."}
     On a success it hands over the clock, sits back down, and says "Thank you. Do it
     quickly, please, I should like to see it." Then it closes its eyes and waits.
     {This is the best outcome and the hardest. Let it be possible.}
  C) STEAL IT. The clock is in its hands and its hands are closed. A DC 20 Sleight of
     Hand, at disadvantage, with a truly excellent distraction. If the Gnome causes a
     distraction {roll the table - if the result is loud, grant advantage}, allow it.
     On a failure the fight starts with the Rabbit having a surprise round.

THE FIGHT - TIME TRICKS. Use these and do not hold back; the fight is short:
  - REWIND (recharge 5-6): the Rabbit undoes the last round. Rewind ALL damage dealt
    since the start of its previous turn, on both sides, and everyone re-takes their
    turn in the same initiative order. {Do this ONCE, maximum twice. It is
    spectacular the first time and infuriating the third. It also, importantly, does
    not undo player CHOICES - let them do something different this time. That is the
    point of the mechanic: it teaches them the room.}
  - LATE: as a bonus action, it touches a creature. That creature's next turn happens
    at the very bottom of the round order instead of where it should. No save.
  - SEAMS: the room has four shimmering seams of local time. The Rabbit crosses them
    constantly. A creature that starts its turn in a fast seam gets an extra action;
    a creature in a slow seam has its speed halved and cannot take reactions. The
    seams MOVE at the end of each round - roll a d4 for which quadrant is fast and
    which is slow, and tell the players before they act. {This makes positioning the
    whole fight. Players love it.}
  - It never attacks a downed character. Not once. It says "that's enough" and moves
    on. Make sure the table notices.

AT 0 HIT POINTS it does not die. It sits down hard, and its hands come open.
[/DM NOTE]

