=== SCENE: THE PIN ===
Goal: the reveal, and the trigger for the whole endgame

[READ ALOUD]
The pocket watch is heavier than it looks and warmer than it should be.

It is beautiful - rose gold, worn smooth, engraved with a pattern of leaves that
someone once loved. The glass is fogged from the inside.

The hands are not moving.

They are TRYING to move. You can feel it through the case, a constant, straining,
insect pressure, like something small and desperate pushing against a door. It has
been pushing for sixty years.

You turn it over.

Driven straight through the back of the case, into the works, bent over on itself and
hammered flat so it cannot slide out, is a hairpin.

It is a little girl's hairpin. Cheap. Pretty. Enamelled with a pattern of daisies.

Scratched along its length, in the wobbly capitals of a child who has just learned to
write her own name and is still very proud of it, it says:

    A L I C E
[/READ ALOUD]

[DM NOTE]
Let this sit. Do not narrate over it. Somebody at the table will say something.

Pulling the pin out takes an action and no roll. Anyone can do it. Ask WHO does it
and write their name down; it matters in Act 6 - the Cheshire will thank that person
specifically and by name, and being thanked by that thing is a mixed blessing.

The Rabbit, watching, says only: "Yes. Go on."

WHEN THE PIN COMES OUT - read the next block immediately, no pause, no questions.
[/DM NOTE]

[READ ALOUD]
The pin comes out with almost no resistance at all. It has been waiting.

The second hand moves one tick.

And every clock in the tower goes off at once.

Not chiming - SCREAMING. Ten thousand bells and cuckoos and gongs and alarms, all
firing simultaneously, all of them sixty years overdue, and the sound is a solid
thing, it is a wall, it is in your chest and behind your eyes.

And the world starts to age.

The gears go orange with rust as you watch. The waistcoat on the rabbit greys and
frays and comes apart at the shoulder. The candles - the ones that never burned
down - go down all at once like a dropped curtain, and the floor of the world, that
endless floor of shoes and plates and keys and hats, dulls and softens and begins to
sag, sixty years arriving all in one place at one time.

Below you, distantly, something enormous made of found objects collapses inward.

And a mile above your heads, the circle of red sky that was your way out closes with
a small polite sound.
[/READ ALOUD]

