=== SCENE: THE GRIN ===
Goal: rescue, in the worst possible shape

[READ ALOUD]
Between one blink and the next, there is a face in the room.

It is enormous. It is thirty feet from ear to ear, and it is not attached to a body,
and it is not attached to anything at all - it is simply THERE, occupying the space
where the far wall was, in front of the gears and behind them at the same time.

It is a cat's face. Grey and green and grinning, and the grin has too many teeth in
it by a factor of about four.

It says - and its voice arrives inside your ribs rather than your ears -

"OH, thank you. Thank you, honestly, genuinely, thank you. Do you know how long I have
been lying on that table? I do. It was one moment. It was the SAME moment. Sixty
years of the same moment, and you have no idea, none, what that is like.

Now. I would run, if I were you, and I am not, and I have never been more relieved
about anything.

This place is coming apart and everything in it is coming apart with it, including
the parts of it that you are standing on and the parts of it that are you."

The grin widens. It keeps widening. It goes on widening past the point where it is a
grin at all, and the mouth opens, and it opens further, and it is a doorway, and it
is wet, and there is a room on the other side of it with mirrors in it.

"In you get."
[/READ ALOUD]

[DM NOTE]
>> CHOICE << and it is a real one - do not push them:

  A) GO INTO THE MOUTH. It is exactly as unpleasant as it sounds. It is warm and it
     smells of pennies and the walk is about four steps and takes about a minute.
     They arrive in the Mirror Lair. No cost. {Ask each player individually. Make
     them each say it out loud. "You. Do you walk into the cat's mouth?"}

  B) REFUSE. Anyone who refuses, the Cheshire regards with real approval, and says:
     "Sensible. Horrid, but sensible. Then find a mirror. Any mirror. Look into it
     and want to be somewhere else more than you want to be here - properly want it,
     not politely - and walk in. Mirrors are doors and always have been. Everyone
     knows that. Everyone just doesn't do it."
     They now need a mirror, at speed, in a collapsing world. THEY HAVE ONE if they
     picked up the Sinister Inventory mirror earlier. If they did not:
       [CHECK] Investigation DC 12, one attempt per character, in the aging debris.
       Success: a hand mirror, tarnished. Failure: 3d6 bludgeoning as a tower of
       fused furniture comes down on them, and they may try once more.
     No character can actually fail their way out of this scene. If they run out of
     rolls, the Rabbit throws them its own shaving mirror without a word.

  C) SOMETHING ELSE ENTIRELY. Teleport, plane shift, dig, climb for the closing brim,
     open a portal. Say YES to all of it - and then have it deliver them to the Mirror
     Lair anyway, because the Cheshire's lair is between everywhere and everywhere
     else and there is nowhere else to arrive from here. Do not make this feel like a
     rail. Let the clever plan work perfectly and land in the same room.

THE RABBIT: it does not come. Ask them if they invite it - most parties do. It says:
"No. Thank you. I have been late for sixty years and I should very much like to be
on time for one thing." And it sits on its stool with its empty hands open and waits
for the ceiling. {If a player physically drags it out, allow it. It survives, it is
inconsolable, and it becomes an NPC for the rest of the campaign who is extremely
useful and extremely sad. That is a good outcome. Do not block it.}
[/DM NOTE]


