== ACT 6 - THE MIRROR LAIR
============================================================

=== SCENE: THE ROOM OF DOORS THAT ARE NOT DOORS ===
Location: nowhere in particular, between everywhere
Present: the Cheshire
Goal: breathe. Give lore. Hand out the gear. Then aim them at Alice.

[map:gilded-stag-upper|The Mirror Lair]

{This is the only safe scene in the chapter. Let it be genuinely calming for about
four minutes before the Cheshire ruins it. Players need one place to sit down.}

[READ ALOUD]
The room is small, and warm, and entirely made of mirrors.

Floor, ceiling, all four walls - mirrors, in every shape: shaving mirrors, wardrobe
mirrors, hand mirrors, a cracked one the size of a barn door, dozens of little oval
ones in tarnished frames. They do not agree about how many of you there are.

There is a rug. There is a low fire in a grate that has no chimney. It is the first
warm, ordinary, kind thing any of you have seen since you left the cave, and it is
very hard not to sit down.

The cat is in the mirrors.

Not in the room - in the MIRRORS, moving between them, appearing in one, crossing
behind your reflection, and stepping out of another one across the room. It is
enormous in some and kitten-sized in others. In one, very briefly, it is nothing but
a long wet red shape on a stone table with its ribs opened out like a book, and then
it moves on and that mirror is empty again.

"Sit," it says. "You have about a quarter of an hour and then it stops being safe,
and I am afraid I am going to spend most of that talking."
[/READ ALOUD]

------------------------------------------------------------

=== SCENE: WHAT THE CAT KNOWS ===
Goal: the lore drop. Deliver it as ANSWERS to their questions, not as a speech.

[DM NOTE]
Do not monologue. Let them ask. Below is everything it will tell them, in the order
it usually comes up. If they do not ask, it does not say. A party that leaves this
room without asking the right questions goes into Act 7 half-blind, and that is a
legitimate consequence of not asking - but the three items in the next scene are
given UNCONDITIONALLY, so no party is ever doomed by it.

ON WHAT THIS PLACE IS:
  "Wonderland is not a place. It is a habit. It runs on imagination the way a mill
   runs on water. Whatever is imagined most FIRMLY here becomes the most true thing
   here. That is the whole physics of it, and it is why everything is so stupid."

ON HER:
  "She was a guest. Do you understand? She fell in like a coin down a drain and we
   were POLITE to her. She was nine. She is still nine. She will always be nine,
   because she never once imagined being anything else, and here that is all it takes.
   She is not strong. She has the arms of a small girl and I could have killed her
   with one paw at any point in the first year.
   She simply believes harder than the rest of us, and that turns out to be the only
   statistic that matters."

ON THE MOUTHS:
  "She likes interesting things. Interesting things get her attention. Her attention
   is a disease. So they made themselves boring, in the only permanent way anyone
   could think of, and it WORKS, and that is the single most damning fact about my
   world."

ON ITSELF:
  "She gutted me on her table looking for something. She did not find it. I am
   extremely pleased about that and it cost me approximately everything."
  {If asked what she was looking for, the cat's grin does not change and it says
  nothing at all for four full seconds and then changes the subject. It knows. It
  will not say tonight. Next chapter.}

ON THE FIRST TRAVELLER:
  "There was a man before her. He mapped all of this. He wrote down what it is and
   how it works and what is on the other side, and he split the record up and hid the
   pieces inside the residents, because we were the only lockboxes he had.
   She has been collecting the pieces ever since. She has all of them but one."

ON THE LAST NOTE - the mission:
  "The last page is in her house. She keeps it close because it is the only object in
   this world that frightens her, and she is a child, and children keep the frightening
   thing under the pillow where they can watch it.
   You want that page. You want it because it says how to LEAVE, and because you are
   going to want to know what it says the moment you are safe, and because as long as
   she has it, nobody else ever gets out. Not you. Not the boy with the hoop."

ON FIGHTING HER - say this slowly and clearly, it is the mission briefing:
  "Do NOT fight her.
   I want to be precise, because you are the sort of people who hear that as a
   difficulty setting. I do not mean it will be hard. I mean it is not a fight. It is
   a category error. She does not have hit points in any sense you would find useful.
   You will hit her and she will decide she was not hit.
   Go in. Do not be interesting. Take the page. Leave.
   If she looks at you - properly LOOKS at you - then you have already made the only
   mistake that was available to you, and the rest is up to how fast you can run."
