=== SCENE: PUZZLE - THE DOLL SHELF ===
Location: between the party and the table with the cloth
Goal: a puzzle under stealth pressure, with a loud failure state

[READ ALOUD]
Between you and the table there is a shelf at floor level, running the length of the
room, and you have to pass along it, and there is no other way that does not cross her
sight line.

On the shelf, sitting in a neat row, there are nine porcelain dolls.

They are identical. Same size, same painted face, same blue dress, same little shoes.
They are facing out. They are facing you.

One of them is not a doll.

You know this the way you know a wasp is in the room. One of them is heavier than a
porcelain doll should be, because one of them is full.
[/READ ALOUD]

[DM NOTE]
THE PUZZLE: identify the mimic among nine, using the brass scales that are sitting on
the shelf beside them {a doll's tea-set balance, big enough for four dolls a side}.

  - All nine look and feel identical.
  - The mimic is HEAVIER. The party knows this - the Cheshire warned them, or a
    [CHECK] Nature DC 10 confirms mimics are denser than what they imitate.
  - You may use the scales TWICE. On the third use the scales creak loudly enough to
    add 1 Notice, and every use after that adds 1 more.
  - Touching a doll to pick it up is safe. Touching THE mimic wakes it. Putting dolls
    on the scale is not touching them enough to matter. {Say this rule out loud.}

THE SOLUTION - two weighings, guaranteed:
  Weighing 1: split into three groups of three. Put group A on the left, group B on
    the right, leave group C on the shelf.
      - If the scales BALANCE, the mimic is in group C.
      - If one side is heavier, the mimic is in that group.
  Weighing 2: take the group of three. Put one doll on the left, one on the right,
    leave one off.
      - Balance means it is the one left off. Otherwise it is the heavy side.
  Two weighings. Nine dolls. Always works.

  {The failure most tables make is weighing one against one, six times. Let them. The
  scales creak. The counter climbs. They will feel the mistake in their spine and
  they will fix it themselves, which is much better than you fixing it for them.}

FOR A CONFIDENT TABLE - the hard version: twelve dolls, and you do NOT know whether
  the mimic is heavier or lighter, and you get three weighings. That is the full
  classic and it is genuinely hard. Offer it only if your players ask for more.

ALTERNATE SOLUTIONS - all of these work, no argument:
  - The UNMIRROR. Burns a charge. Instant. This is a completely valid use and it is
    why they have three charges; spending one here is a real cost for Act 8.
  - The GNOME. Two or three yes/no questions and it is solved. Also completely valid.
    {If they use the Gnome here, quietly enjoy it: they have now used the same tool
    to solve four different puzzles tonight, and that is the arc of the Gnome.}
  - Detect Magic, a familiar sent to touch each, a thrown pebble, a druid's sense of
    smell. Say yes. Say yes to all of it.
  - Simply not going along the shelf: crawling under it, going over, or across the
    rug in her sight line with the Grin under their tongues. Allow it. It is a DC 16
    group Stealth check and failure adds 2 Notice and starts the mimics anyway.

IF THEY WAKE IT: see the encounter below. Add 1 Notice immediately.
[/DM NOTE]

