[READ ALOUD]
The table is fourteen feet long and the cloth over it is heavy and stained in exactly
one place, an old brown stain about the size of a wagon wheel, right in the middle.

Under the cloth, the shape is long, and low, and it has not moved in sixty years,
and it does not smell of anything at all, which is the wrongest part.

Pinned to the cloth with a hairpin - a cheap, pretty one, enamelled with daisies,
identical to the one you took out of the clock - there is a single sheet of paper.

It is folded twice. It is very old. The ink is brown.

You can read the first line without unfolding it:

    "If you are reading this, you are not the first, and I am so terribly sorry."
[/READ ALOUD]

[DM NOTE]
DO NOT LET THEM READ THE REST TONIGHT. If they try, the words move. Every time. It is
