=== ENCOUNTER: RUN ===
Enemies: Alice (Appendix C), and the house
Terrain / hazards: all of it
Tactics: she does not chase. The house does. She strolls.
Treasure: their lives
Scaling: see below - this fight has a fixed ending and cannot be lost

[DM NOTE]
READ THIS BEFORE YOU RUN IT. This encounter is a SCRIPTED THREE ROUNDS. It is not a
fight the party can win, and it is not a fight the party can lose, and you must run
it so that they believe both of those things are false.

  ROUND 1 - THE HOUSE COMES. Every mimic in the room animates: use 8 mimics, or as
    many as you can be bothered to track, and treat them as a hazard rather than
    creatures - at the end of each character's turn, if they are within 10 feet of any
    furniture, DC 14 Dex save or take 2d6 and be grabbed (escape DC 13).
    Alice does nothing at all this round. She is choosing.

  ROUND 2 - SHE PLAYS. On her turn she does exactly ONE of these, chosen for maximum
    theatre, and it does not miss and it does not allow a save, because she is not
    casting anything, she is deciding:
      - "You're a chair." One character becomes a chair until the end of Alice's next
        turn. They can see and hear. They cannot act. They are a nice chair.
      - "That's not yours." The Last Note is in her hand. It was in their hand. It is
        now in her hand. {If she does this, the party must take it back to escape -
        see below - which is a superb complication. Use it if the fight feels flat.}
      - "Down." Every character within 30 feet is prone and takes 3d6 bludgeoning as
        the floor becomes briefly, sincerely, the ceiling.
      - She simply walks up to whoever is most interesting, takes their hand, and
        says "come and see". If nobody stops her, that character is at 0 hit points.
        Anyone may stop her by being MORE interesting, immediately, out loud, with a
        real line from the player. If the player says something genuinely good, it
        works, and she turns to them instead, and now it is their hand she is holding.

  ROUND 3 - THE GNOME.
    At the top of round 3 - before anyone acts, before initiative, before anything -
    stop the table. Then read THE ENDING, below.

WHAT IF THE PARTY IS WINNING? They are not. Any damage dealt to Alice is described as
landing and then not having landed. "The axe goes into her shoulder and she looks at
it, and then it hasn't." Do this once, coldly, and the table will understand the
category of thing they are dealing with.

WHAT IF A CHARACTER DIES? Nobody dies here. Characters go to 0 and are stable. She
does not kill things she is enjoying. Death saves are made at advantage in this room
because she is, in her way, being careful with her toys. {If your table has a
character who has been begging for a heroic death all campaign - let it happen here,
and let it be the reason the others get out, and give them the biggest moment in the
chapter. Otherwise: nobody dies.}
[/DM NOTE]

