-- THE FIVE PUZZLES AND THEIR ANSWERS, FOR FAST LOOKUP --

  ACT 2 - THE TWO WARDENS (one always lies, one always tells the truth, one question)
    Answer: ask EITHER warden "which gate would the OTHER one tell me to take?" and
    then take the other gate. Any question that routes through both wardens works.
    Accept any working formulation instantly, including weird ones. Fail forward: the
    wrong gate is not fatal, it is merely much longer and costs them the Act 3 head
    start.

  ACT 3 - THE GAME OF COURT (five cards, back to front, call your own suit)
    Answer: the back player counts the hearts he can see and calls "hearts" if the
    count is odd, "spades" if even. Everyone forward of him tracks the parity and
    deduces their own card. Four of five are always saved; the back player is a coin
    flip. The Queen's card is the front one, and the front player is therefore the
    one who is guaranteed to live, which is a fact worth pointing out afterwards.

  ACT 4 - THE EIGHT TEACUPS (find the poisoned one, three yes/no questions)
    Answer: binary search. "Is it in cups 1-4?" halves it. Then 1-2. Then 1. Three
    questions, eight cups, always. Optional puzzle; solving it means the Hatter drinks
    the poison and starts the fight at half hit points.

  ACT 5 - THE TWO ROPES (each burns one hour, unevenly, measure 45 minutes)
    Answer: light rope A at BOTH ends and rope B at one end simultaneously. When A is
    gone, 30 minutes have passed; at that instant light B's other end; B's remaining
    30 minutes of rope burns in 15. 30 + 15 = 45.

  ACT 5 - THE LANTERN BRIDGE (walkers of 1/2/5/10 minutes, one lantern, two at a time,
    someone must walk it back)
    Answer, 17 minutes: 1 and 2 cross (2), 1 returns (1), 5 and 10 cross (10), 2
    returns (2), 1 and 2 cross (2). Total 17.
    The intuitive answer of always sending the fastest as courier gives 19. Both get
    them across; 17 gets them across before the landing folds.

  ACT 7 - THE NINE DOLLS (one is a heavier mimic, two weighings)
    Answer: three groups of three. Weigh group A vs group B; balance means it is in C,
    otherwise it is in the heavy group. Then from that group of three, weigh one vs
    one; balance means it is the one left off. Two weighings, always.

