puzzle the players can HOLD is a different puzzle from one they have to remember you
saying, and every one of these is a puzzle they will otherwise ask you to repeat four
times. Hand them over face down. Do not paraphrase them. Do not add anything.}

------------------------------------------------------------
HANDOUT 1 - MARGARET'S SLATE                    {Act 2, give one line at a time}
------------------------------------------------------------

   We are not permitted to be interesting.

   She hears clever things. Do not be clever near her.

   The Queen's castle takes people in. Some of them.
   If your papers are in order.

   Do not go to the tea house. Nobody comes back from a party.

   The cat is dead. She has it on the table.
   She keeps it dead ON PURPOSE.

{Write these on an actual slate or a scrap of card and rub each one out before you
show the next. Never let two lines exist at the same time. The erasing is the scene.}

------------------------------------------------------------
HANDOUT 2 - THE WARDENS' RULES                  {Act 2, hand over and shut up}
------------------------------------------------------------

   TWO DOORS. ONE IS THE WAY IN.

   ONE OF US ALWAYS TELLS THE TRUTH.
   ONE OF US ALWAYS LIES.

   YOU MAY ASK ONE OF US ONE QUESTION.
   IT MUST BE ANSWERABLE YES OR NO.

   ONE QUESTION. NOT ONE EACH. ONE.

   GUESS WRONG AND YOU GO IN THE KENNEL.

{Put this card on the table and then say nothing at all for as long as it takes. Do
not hint. Do not rescue them. The Gnome is sitting right there and one of them will
eventually look at it, and that moment is worth the silence.}

------------------------------------------------------------
HANDOUT 3 - THE RULES OF COURT                  {Act 3, one card per player}
------------------------------------------------------------

   FIVE PLAYERS STAND IN A LINE, FACING THE THRONE.

   EACH WEARS ONE CARD UPON THE BROW.

   YOU SEE EVERY CARD IN FRONT OF YOU.
   YOU SEE NOTHING BEHIND YOU.
   YOU NEVER SEE YOUR OWN.

   BEGINNING AT THE BACK, EACH OF YOU WILL NAME
   ONE SUIT ALOUD. THAT IS YOUR GUESS AT YOUR OWN CARD.

   YOU MAY NOT SPEAK OTHERWISE. NOT ONE WORD.
   YOU MAY NOT SIGNAL. NOT ONE GESTURE.

   NAME YOUR SUIT CORRECTLY AND YOU LIVE.

   YOU MAY CONFER BEFORE THE CARDS ARE PLACED.
   YOU MAY NOT CONFER AFTER.

{The last two lines are the whole puzzle and players skim past them every time. Read
those two lines out loud a second time after handing the card over, and then let the
conferring happen. Twenty minutes of table argument here is a feature.}

------------------------------------------------------------
HANDOUT 4 - THE ROPES AND THE BRIDGE            {Act 5, both on one card}
------------------------------------------------------------

   TWO ROPES. EACH BURNS THROUGH IN EXACTLY ONE HOUR.
   NEITHER BURNS EVENLY. HALF A ROPE IS NOT HALF AN HOUR.
   YOU HAVE FIRE. MEASURE FORTY-FIVE MINUTES.

   ------

   FOUR OF YOU MUST CROSS. ONE LANTERN.
   THE BRIDGE HOLDS TWO AT A TIME AND NO MORE.
   THE LANTERN CANNOT BE THROWN, SLID, OR SENT ALONE.
   WHOEVER CROSSES, CROSSES AT THE SLOWER ONE'S PACE.
   ONE MINUTE. TWO MINUTES. FIVE MINUTES. TEN MINUTES.
   THE LANDING FOLDS IN EIGHTEEN.

{Eighteen is the number to say out loud. The intuitive solution takes nineteen. They
will find nineteen, be pleased, be told it is not enough, and go quiet - and the two
minutes after that are the best problem-solving of the night.}

------------------------------------------------------------
HANDOUT 5 - THE DOLL SHELF                      {Act 7, whisper it}
------------------------------------------------------------

   NINE DOLLS ON A SHELF.

   EIGHT OF THEM WEIGH THE SAME.
   ONE IS HEAVIER. THE HEAVY ONE IS AWAKE.

   THERE IS A SET OF BRASS SCALES.

   IT WILL BEAR TWO WEIGHINGS BEFORE
   THE NOISE OF IT REACHES HER.

{Say the last two lines rather than only writing them. Then put the card down and put
your hands in your lap, and let them work in whispers, because they will start
whispering on their own and you must not be the one who asked them to.}

------------------------------------------------------------
HANDOUT 6 - THE LAST NOTE                       {Act 7. THE ONLY ONE THEY KEEP}
------------------------------------------------------------

{Make this one properly. Real paper, tear the edges, fold it four times, age it with
tea if you are that sort of DM. It is the object the entire chapter is named after and
they are going to carry it into the next one.

They may look at the FIRST LINE ONLY. Hold the rest folded under your thumb. If a
player asks to read on, say - out loud, in your own voice, not a character's -
"not in here." That is the whole rule and it is not negotiable.}

   THE FRONT, IN A NEAT AND CHEERFUL HAND:

      The last note. Do not read this where she can hear you.

   {AND NOTHING ELSE. NOT TONIGHT.}

